\version "2.26.0"
% c598 (lens 8, A&S Unit 21 PDF 349 sec. 1-2: preparation, suspension and resolution are one figure; PDF 350 sec. 3-4: the resolution is a relaxation, softer than the suspension): 49 Cl 1 and Hn 1 slur the 9-8 (B4 tied over the A bass) into its resolution A4; the slur used to end on the tied B, so both players re-tongued the resolution (Hn 1 with a tenuto) mid-bar. Cl 1's next slur now starts on the leap to E5 (beat 3), Hn 1's at 50. Notes, dynamics and MIDI unchanged.
% c564 (lens 4, Rimsky-Korsakov appendix PDF 505-507): 56, violas keep D4 (was G4), so the G-major close before the fugato is a complete chord (it was G and B only), as 132 and 288 keep their fifth B3 in Cl 2 and violas.
% c459 (lens 9, Adler PDF 139 Ex. V-16, PDF 141 Ex. V-19): the strings' answers in the counterstatement (31-32, 35-36) are doubled an octave lower by the cellos, arco spiccato, as Shostakovich 6/iii doubles the violins with the cellos when they reach their low register and Haydn 103 sets off the second statement by adding the cellos; the double basses hold the bass arco pp under each answer (31-32, 35-36), so the octave doubling sits over a sustained root instead of becoming the bass itself, as the wind chords do under the calls (Adler PDF 245, Ex. VIII-12: a held bass points up a spiccato tune by contrast). Cellos and basses stay arco to 45 (downbeats 37-44 staccato).
% c452 (lens 2, A&S Unit 5 PDF 76, 81-82, 87-88; Ex. 5-11b, 5-12, 5-29): the applied leading tone in the bass now rises a semitone in its own register into the hemiola 6/4 (cellos C sharp 3-D3 at 52-53, was a falling major seventh to D2; A sharp an octave lower at 128/284 in Vc/Bsn 2 A sharp 2 and Cb/Cbsn A sharp 1, was A sharp 3/A sharp 2 falling a major seventh to B). The bass meets the tune falling to 1 in contrary motion, and the falling sixth F sharp-A sharp turns back by step (PDF 81).
% c451 (lens 1, A&S Unit 20 PDF 334 Ex. 20-8, PDF 335-336 neighbour figure, PDF 340 Ex. 20-24): the second idea's cadential phrase opens with an upper neighbour and a passing tone instead of the arpeggio (G: d'''4 e'''8 d''' c'''4 was d'''4 b''4 g''4 at 53; E minor: b''4 cis'''8 b'' a''4 was b''4 g''4 e''4 at 129 and 285), so the top line falls 5-4-3-2-1 by step into the hemiola's 3-2-1 instead of reaching 1 a bar early; the bar takes motive x's rhythm, legato. Raised 6 (C sharp) over V in minor (Ex. 20-24; no C-E-G, c091). Ob 1 at 129/285 takes the upper octave from the downbeat (was B4 leaping to G5 on beat 2). Tenor doubling at 285 (Vc, Cl 2, Hn 2) keeps the frame tones (Ex. 20-27 footnote).
% c548 (lens 8, A&S Unit 3 PDF 53-54, Ex. 3-13: a phrase is a group of bars whose end meets a goal of tonal motion; PDF 50: the A6 is the idea's built-in register accent):
%   the G-major second idea no longer falls back to f after its second peak. 49-52 is one crescendo (strings f -> ff at 53, winds mf -> f, bass f -> ff with Bsn, Cbsn, Cb),
%   so the second four-bar group leads into the cadence group 53-56 instead of repeating the first group's swell; c448's swell into the 9-8 at 54-55 stays, one level higher
%   (hidden fff/ff in the strings, ff/f in the winds). This is the shape the E-minor statements already have (125-129, 281-285: crescendo through the second phrase to f).
%   Violas leave the tune at 52.2 inside the crescendo (the mf there removed) and reach ff with the other strings at 53. Printed fff stays only at 336.
% c448 (lens 8, A&S Unit 3 PDF 51, 55, Ex. 3-6, 3-16-18): the second idea's cadential hemiola (54-55, 130-131, 286-287) now swells into the tied 9-8
%   (\< over the hemiola's first minim, peak on the dissonant downbeat, \> to the resolution on beat 2), in every voice that carries the tune (Vln I/II, Fl 1, Ob 1;
%   reprise also Vc, Cl 1/2, Hn 2 [Hn 2 shape only, level kept at f]). c441 turned the cadence into a suspension; every other melodic suspension in the movement
%   is shaded this way (T 155-156, 159-160, 165-166, 199-200; c348 row 13, c398). Hidden levels close each hairpin for the MIDI (c134).
% c432 (A&S Units 30-31, PDF 537, 554, 556, Ex. 31-3b, 31-6): 48 beats 2-3 the second idea's first phrase ends on E7 in 6/5 (bass G sharp 2 in Bsn 1, Cbsn, Vc, Cb; Hn 2 E3 for the whole bar; Cl 1/Hn 1 B4; Cl 2 D4 the 7th, resolving to C sharp 4 at 49), prepared by Cl 1/Hn 1 G4-G sharp 4 over the E bass on 48 b1 (one voice, so the G sharp is not doubled and passes to the bass on beat 2): the bass rises G sharp-A into 49 as it rises C sharp-D into 53, so both phrases of the sequence end on an applied leading tone (was B2, G/B).
% c419: 45-52 the second idea in a third octave: the violas (f espress., with the violins' slurs and hairpins, up-bow at 45 so the hemiola 53 starts down-bow as c218 bowed it) play the tune an octave below Vln II/Ob 1, joining Cl 2, which already had it at 47-48 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 78: melody 'in three and four octaves', Ex. 87 PDF 261 lowest octave violas with clarinets; PDF 76-77 and Ex. 80 PDF 253: violas + clarinet). They return to the A7's seventh G4 at 52 beat 2 (c102, mf) and to their harmony for the hemiola cadence 53-56. Tested with the cor anglais as well: the build went to 14 pages, so violas alone.
% c411: second idea, first minim of each phrase (45, 49, 121, 125, 277, 281): dotted crotchet + passing quaver inside the minim (d'''4. c'''8 b''4~ was d'''2 b''4~), filling the chordal third before the rising sixth. The tie over the bar line and the three-minim hemiola stay (attacks still on beats 1, 3, 2); the quaver is unaccented, as the lead-on quaver at 48/52 (Sebesky PDF 210, Ex. 277-278: a long-value lead line needs a figure of its own; PDF 206, Ex. 270: a recurring figure unifies).
% c268: 28 timpani p printed, as at 20: the same answer after the same 7-bar rest carried p at 20 and nothing at 28 (MOLA PDF 10, Ex. 3: an entry after a multi-bar rest carries its dynamic). Also 268 (dA).
% c218: 51-52 Vln I/II end the slur before the anticipating quaver (e'''4.) d'''8), violas d'4) g'2, so the hemiola 53-55 starts down-bow again and its minims at 54 and 55 fall on down-bows as c085 bowed them (c118's up-bow at 45 had turned the whole passage over); Adler PDF 38. 45-46 violas' repeated G under the slur get louré dashes (Adler PDF 42).
% c143: theme A bar 10 (22, 114, 262): the bass neighbour A-G-A under iv becomes A-G sharp-A, V6 of iv (E major over G sharp) instead of i6 (A-S Unit 18 PDF 297: in minor the neighbouring I6 to IV is "generally altered to form an applied dominant, V6 of IV"; a bass neighbour works best by half step). The leading tone G sharp is only in the bass line (A-S PDF 293); inner parts take B or E. 314 is unchanged: its G is passing (F sharp-G-A, c058).
% c152: 38 the tail on G is spelled B-B flat-A-G (was B-A sharp-A-G): a descending chromatic passing tone is a lowered note a minor 2nd above its goal (A-S PDF 497, Ex. 28-23), and in major
%   the preferred chromatics are sharp 4 and flat 7, not the remote sharp 2 (PDF 497-498, Ex. 28-24, 28-26); as 42 (C-B-B flat-A) and the G-minor fugato's B flat at 58. Sound unchanged.
% SCHERZO, first part, bars 13-56 (E minor).
% c085: 53-55 hemiola rebowed (Adler PDF 38-39): Vln d-(b g) | a2 (fis | a) g2, so each hemiola minim falls on a down-bow and no 7-note slur at f; Vla repeated notes as louré (dashes under the slur, Adler PDF 42).
% c035: 44 Ob 1·2 apart for one bar: the unison crotchet shares a notehead with two stems (Gould PDF 65) instead of the crowded "a2 1." label.
% c022: 19 viola B (was D sharp): no augmented 2nd C-D sharp from iv to V7 (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 166, 169); 47 b3-48 b1 viola G (was E): the leading tone rises and the 3rd of vi is doubled in V-vi (A-S PDF 202-203).
% 13-28: theme A in Vln I, p leggiero, over pizzicato bass and off-beat crotchets in Vln II and violas. Motive x = crotchet, two quavers, crotchet.
%   The tail G-F sharp-F-E (14-15, 26-27) is the motto's chromatic tail (E flat-D-D flat-C in C) on E's own degrees; F natural is E's Neapolitan, as D flat is C's.
%   Timpani answer at 20 and 28. Cadences: half cadence 16, authentic 20 and 28 (E minor is the key; no C major chord anywhere in the scherzo).
% 29-44: counterstatement as call and response: winds alone (string-free) at 29-30, 33-34; strings answer 31-32, 35-36; together from 37 in G major, on V of G 41-44.
% 45-56: second idea in G major (III), forte, legato, in hemiola (three minims across two bars) against the horns' dotted minims; cadence in G 53-55.

% c032: 49-52 bass A-C#-A | E-F#-C# (was A-A-G | F#-F#-C#): phrase 2 of the second idea now repeats phrase 1's bass a step higher, so the
%   outer voices keep phrase 1's pattern 5-8-8 | 10-8-10 (were 5-10-9 | 9-8-10: parallel ninths A/G -> G/F#); 51 is A7/E -> D/F# (V4/3 -> V6 of V).
%   Cl 2 and violas re-voiced (no octaves with the new bass). A-S Unit 17, PDF 268-269, 287.
% c054: 29-30 and 33-34 Fl 1 plays the wind call at Vln I's pitch, in unison with Ob 1 (was an octave above them, up to G6 and A6 at p):
%   flute and oboe in unison give a rounder tone than the oboe alone (Adler PDF 236, Ex. VIII-2), the winds echo the strings in their own register
%   (PDF 233, function 3), and the flute does not reach A6 in this soft answer (it first does at the f second idea, 50, then at 110 and 219), so it is not spent in bar 34 (PDF 238, Schubert 8/i).
% c076: 44 Fl 1 rests on beat 3 (was D5), as Vln I does since c016: Fl 1 played 37-55 (about 13 s, f) with no rest, the only breath a stolen quaver at 48/49; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 37: wood-wind players must be given a little rest from time to time. Ob 1 keeps its D4.
% c278: 29 "leggiero" for Fl 1 and Ob 1, the winds' first statement of the head (the strings have it at 13, Fl 1 at 253): Adler PDF 189 (Mendelssohn 4/iv), 201 (Berlioz, Symphonie fantastique v) print p leggiero on fast wind staccato; PDF 211: the oboe's staccato is the most pointed of the woodwinds.
% c368: 45 "espress." on Fl 1 and Ob 1, which double the violins' second idea (marked espress.), and f on Vln II at 45 as on Vln I: every staff that carries the tune carries its word and level (MOLA PDF 8: vertical and horizontal lines proofread alike; Ex. 6, PDF 11: the marks restated in every staff).
% c504 (lens 4, Adler ch. 8): 37-40 Ob 1 (not Fl 2) joins Fl 1 and Vln I on the theme, p <, so the 'together' bars combine the call's own colour (Fl 1 + Ob 1, 29-30/33-34)
%   with the strings' answer (Adler I PDF 253, Schumann 1/iii: choirs heard apart, then together; PDF 270, Copland: the third statement sums a strings-alone and a winds-alone
%   phrase; PDF 236, Ex. VIII-2: flute with oboe is rounder than either). Fl 2 rests to 41 and enters f a2 with Ob 2 and the cor anglais: a soft line is not given to two like
%   winds in unison (PDF 249-250), and the crescendo into 41 is made by entries (PDF 238, Schubert's flute). Supersedes c107's Fl 2 a2 at 37-40 (c107's held notes stay gone).
sAFluteOne = { R2.*16 | e''4-.\p^\markup \italic "leggiero" b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | g''2->( fis''8 f''8 | e''4-.) r4 r4 | R2. | e''4-. b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | a''2->( gis''8 g''8 | fis''4-.) r4 r4 | R2. | g''4-.\p\< d''8-. d''-. g''4-. | b''2->-\sysLabel "1."( bes''8 a''8 | g''4-.) d'''8-. d'''-. b''4-. | a''4-. fis''-. d''-. | a''4-.\f d''8-. d''-. a''4-. | c'''2->( b''8 bes''8 | a''4-.) fis''8-. fis''-. d''4-. | a''4-. fis''-. r4 | d'''4.->(\mf^\markup \italic "espress."\< c'''8 b''4~ | b''4 g'''2-\tweak stencil ##f \f\> | fis'''2 e'''4~ | e'''4-\tweak stencil ##f \mf d'''4. fis'''8) | e'''4.->(\< d'''8 cis'''4~ | cis'''4 a'''2 | g'''2 fis'''4~ | fis'''4 e'''4. d'''8) | d'''4(\f e'''8 d''' c'''4 | b''2\< a''4~ | a''4-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\> g''2-\tweak stencil ##f \f) | g''4-. r4 r4 | }  % c036: 40 and 44 the falling arpeggio an octave up (was A4-F sharp 4-D4, the flute's weakest octave, under a f tutti; Adler PDF 185-186), so 44-45 is an octave, not D4-D6
% c107: 37-44 Fl 2, and 41-43 Ob 2, play the theme a2 with their firsts instead of holding D5 / F sharp 5 / D5 inside the theme's compass: the held note met the theme's
%   quavers in unison in the same colour and Fl 1 crossed below it at 39 (Adler I PDF 135: background out of the melody's register, above all in like colour; PDF 137, 152: add
%   instruments to the melody for the crescendo). The D7 at 41-43 stays complete below the theme (Cl, Hn, Bsn, Vln II, Vla). Also 273-275 (dA).
sAFluteTwo = { R2.*16 | R2.*12 | a''4-.\f d''8-. d''-. a''4-. | c'''2->( b''8 bes''8 | a''4-.) fis''8-. fis''-. d''4-. | a''4-. fis''-. r4 | R2.*12 | }  % c504: Fl 2 now first enters f at 41 (Ob 1 takes 37-40). Earlier, c055: Fl 2's first note carried p (Adler II PDF 301, Ex. XVIII-10: each player of a shared staff has his own dynamics)
sAOboeOne = { R2.*16 | e''4-.\p^\markup \italic "leggiero" b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | g''2->( fis''8 f''8 | e''4-.) r4 r4 | R2. | e''4-. b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | a''2->( gis''8 g''8 | fis''4-.) r4 r4 | R2. | g''4-.\p\< d''8-. d''-. g''4-. | b''2->-\sysLabel "1."( bes''8 a''8 | g''4-.) d'''8-. d'''-. b''4-. | a''4-. fis''-. d''-. | a''4-.\f d''8-. d''-. a''4-. | c'''2->( b''8 bes''8 | a''4-.) fis''8-. fis''-. d''4-. | a'4-. r4 r4 | d''4.->(\mf^\markup \italic "espress."\< c''8 b'4~ | b'4 g''2-\tweak stencil ##f \f\> | fis''2 e''4~ | e''4-\tweak stencil ##f \mf d''4. fis''8) | e''4.->(\< d''8 cis''4~ | cis''4 a''2 | g''2 fis''4~ | fis''4 e''4. d''8) | d''4(\f e''8 d'' c''4 | b'2\< a'4~ | a'4-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\> g'2-\tweak stencil ##f \f) | g'4-. r4 r4 | }
% c086: 44 the falling A4-F sharp 4-D4 goes to the cor anglais (Ob 1 keeps the A4 with Ob 2): the oboe's D4 on beat 3 stood alone in the bottom of its range,
%   which Sebesky calls coarse and the hardest to control (PDF 71: F down to B flat, wise to avoid); the cor anglais is not coarse there and serves as the low voice in place of an oboe (PDF 72).
% c367: 41-43 the cor anglais plays theme A's head an octave under the six-part unison (Fl 1-2, Ob 1-2, Vln I-II), leading into its own falling arpeggio at 44 (c086): Sebesky PDF 140 rule 6 (a strong concerted passage is reinforced an octave lower), PDF 151-152 (Ex. 202-203), PDF 165 (Ex. 213: winds inside the string voicing an octave under the lead). As the tutti does at 105-112 (C.a. and Cl 2). Also 273-275 (dA). The violas keep the off-beats.
% c471 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 60, Sheherazade iii [M]: Vn I + Fl / Vn II + Ob / lower octave + Eng. horn; PDF 59: Violas + Eng. horn among the best unisons; PDF 68: Ob over C.a. in octaves 'always a good effect'):
%   45-55 the cor anglais takes the second idea an octave under Ob 1, with the violas to 52 and alone on that octave through the hemiola cadence 53-55, so each of the three octaves has its reed (c419 had dropped it for a page split; the build stays 13 pages)
sACorAnglais = { R2.*28 | a'4-.\f d'8-. d'-. a'4-. | c''2->( b'8 bes'8 | a'4-.) fis'8-. fis'-. d'4-. | a'4-. fis'-. d'-. | d'4.->(\mf^\markup \italic "espress."\< c'8 b4~ | b4 g'2-\tweak stencil ##f \f\> | fis'2 e'4~ | e'4-\tweak stencil ##f \mf d'4. fis'8) | e'4.->(\< d'8 cis'4~ | cis'4 a'2 | g'2 fis'4~ | fis'4 e'4. d'8) | d'4(\f e'8 d' c'4 | b2\< a4~ | a4-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\> g2-\tweak stencil ##f \f) | g4-. r4 r4 | }
sAOboeTwo = { R2.*16 | R2.*12 | a''4-.\f d''8-. d''-. a''4-. | c'''2->( b''8 bes''8 | a''4-.) fis''8-. fis''-. d''4-. | R2. | R2.*12 | }
% c122: 52 Cl 1 E5 on the second minim (was C sharp 5): A7/C sharp (V6/5 of V), the last chord of the second idea's sequence before the cadence in G,
%   no longer doubles its bass, the applied leading tone (A-S PDF 267 and 287: a doubled leading tone is acceptable inside a sequence but not just before its goal;
%   PDF 76; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 83, as c094 at 51). Cl 1 rises C sharp-D-E and falls to B4 at 53.
% c172: 55 the second idea's G-major cadence: Vln II/Ob 1's appoggiatura A4 no longer sounds between held G4 (Cl 2, violas: its tone of resolution) and B4 (Cl 1, Hn 1). Cl 1 holds A4 from 54 and rises to B4, Hn 1 C5-B4 (4-3 in thirds over the A-G), Cl 2 and violas D4, as c139 did at 131/287 (A-S Unit 27 PDF 469-470, Unit 21 sec. 12: the resolution tone should not sound in a voice other than the bass; PDF 474 Ex. 27-16a: no 3rd under the 4th).
sAClOne = { R2.*16 | g'2.~\pp | g'2. | g'4 r4 r4 | R2. | a'2.~ | a'2. | fis'4 r4 r4 | R2. | b'2.~\< | b'2.~ | b'2. | a'2. | c''2.~\f | c''2. | a'2. | fis'4 r4 r4 | b'2(\mf d''4~ | d''4 b'2) | a'2( g'4 | gis'4 b'2~ | b'4 a'4) e''4~( | e''4 e''2) | cis''2( d''4~ | d''4 e''2) | b'4( d''4 b'4 | b'2 c''4~ | c''4 b'2) | b'4-. r4 r4 | }
% c052: 45 Cl 2 B3 (was G4) for the first minim: Vln II's C4, the seventh of V6/5 at 44, resolves to B3 in the next voice and the same register (A-S PDF 399, 406-407).
% c094: 41-42 (and 273-274) Cl 2 A4 (was F sharp 4, a tritone under Cl 1's held C5 and in unison with Hn 1): the clarinet pair holds thirds C-A, A-F sharp (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 90 rule 4).
% c094: 51 Cl 2 and violas A4 (were E4): the bass E of V4/3 (A7/E) is not doubled in the upper parts (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 83: the bass of an inversion of the dominant seventh is never doubled above).
% c370 (cyclic lens; A&S Unit 23, PDF 388: flat 6 is active toward 5 and should arrive on it): at 41-43 the motto tail on D (C-B-Bb-A) is now doubled by the cor anglais an octave lower (c367),
% so Cl 2 leaves the held A4 at the second bar for F#4: the tail's Bb4 no longer sounds against its own goal A4 at the same pitch, and the A arrives as the tail's resolution (A3 stays in Bsn 1).
% c472 (lens 2, A&S Unit 12 PDF 185, Ex. 12-20e '6/4 chord avoids 5ths', PDF 182 Ex. 12-8): 54 Cl 2 and violas hold G4 (the 6/4's 4th) to beat 3 and fall to F sharp 4 with the melody's 6-5 (B-A) and the horns' change, so the cadential 6/4 resolves to V7 as one chord on the hemiola's second minim; with c471's cor anglais C4-B3 (53.3-54.1) the old G4-F sharp 4 on the downbeat made parallel fifths C-G / B-F sharp.
sAClTwo = { R2.*16 | e'2.~\pp | e'2. | e'4 r4 r4 | R2. | e'2.~ | e'2. | d'4 r4 r4 | R2. | g'2.~\< | g'2.~ | g'2. | fis'2. | a'2.\f | fis'2.~ | fis'2. | d'4 r4 r4 | b2(\mf g'4~ | g'4 g'2) | fis'2( e'4~ | e'4 d'2) | cis'2( a'4~ | a'4 e'2) | a'2.~( | a'4 a'2) | g'4( g'4 g'4 | g'2 fis'4 | d'2.) | g'4-. r4 r4 | }
% c294: 53-56 Bsn 1 D3-D3-G3-G3 (was D2-D2-G2-G2 in unison with Vc, Cb and Cbn): the forte cadential 6/4 had nothing between the bass D2 and D4
%   (two empty octaves); every tutti chord in Rimsky-Korsakov's appendix (PDF 505-507) fills the octave above the bass, and PDF 125
%   warns against widely separated parts without the intermediate octave; as Bsn 1 B3/E3 over the bass at 129-131 and 285-287. Bsn 1 now steps C#3-D3.
sABsnOne = { R2.*16 | b2.~\pp | b2. | b4 r4 r4 | R2. | c'2.~ | c'2. | a4 r4 r4 | R2. | d'2.~\< | d'2.~ | d'2. | d'2. | a2.~\f | a2. | c'2. | a4 r4 r4 | g,2(\f g4~ | g4 b,2) | d2( e4~ | e4 gis,2) | a,2(\< a4~ | a4 cis2) | e2( d4~ | d4 cis2) | d2.~\ff | d2. | g2. | g4-. r4 r4 | }
sABsnTwo = { R2.*16 | e2.~\pp | e2. | e4 r4 r4 | R2. | a,2.~ | a,2. | d4 r4 r4 | R2. | g,2.~\< | g,2.~ | g,2. | d2. | d2.~\f | d2.~ | d2. | d4 r4 r4 | g,2(\f g4~ | g4 b,2) | d2( e4~ | e4 gis,2) | a,2(\< a4~ | a4 cis2) | e2( d4~ | d4 cis2) | d2.~\ff | d2. | g,2. | g,4-. r4 r4 | }
sACbsn = { R2.*32 | g,,2(\f g,4~ | g,4 b,,2) | d,2( e,4~ | e,4 gis,,2) | a,,2(\< a,4~ | a,4 cis,2) | e,2( d,4~ | d,4 cis,2) | d,2.~\ff | d,2. | g,,2. | g,,4-. r4 r4 | }
% c397: 45 Hn 1-2 mf reprinted at the second idea's first chord, a new phrase after the rests of 44 (Adler II PDF 50, 52: every entry marked).
% c088: 45-56 Hn 1-2 sustained chords in four-bar phrase slurs with tenuto on the repeated notes (portato = soft, legato tonguing 'da', Adler I PDF 309; Ex. X-16 four horns under slurs, 'sehr weich'), matching the second idea's phrases.
% c147: 41-44 (and 273-276, dA.ily) Hn 1-2 mf, a degree under the woodwind's f (were f): in the D7 under the a2 theme the two horns held the root and third
%   while the seventh C5 and A4 lay in single clarinets (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108-109: played forte, the horns are more powerful than the wood-wind; PDF 112: mark the
%   wood-wind one degree louder than the brass). The repeated mf at 45 is removed (the level carries on).
% c149: the horns enter pp and swell to the mf level by 43 (275 in dA): after 34 bars rest (Hn 2 has not played yet) an mf entry is the colourless re-entry Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 136 warns against (pp or ff); Ex. 304, 298, 312 bring the horns in pp. The level from 43 is c147s mf.
% c262: 47 Hn 1 A4-G4 and Hn 2 F sharp 3-G3 on beat 3 (were held to 48): over the bass E at 47 b3 Hn 1's A4 sounded a tone above its own resolution G4 in Cl 1 and violas (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 469-470, Ex. 27-10; PDF 352), as c139/c172 removed at 55, 131 and 196; the horns now make the V-vi move with the strings (leading tone F sharp up, as Hn 2 at 123-124 does in E minor).
% c464 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 102, 106, 108): 54-56 Hn 1-2 take the G-major cadence's suspensions as tied thirds, A4/C5 from 54 beat 3 (Hn 2 with Ob 1/Vln II's A4, Hn 1 with Cl 1's C5) resolving to G4/B4 at 55 beat 2,
%   as Hn 2-3 do at 130-131 (F sharp 4/A4 -> E4/G4) and 286-287 in E minor. Was Hn 1 B4-A4 | C5 (struck) over Hn 2 D4 held: the seventh D4-C5 lay inside one horn colour on the suspension beat (PDF 102: discords of the 7th to
%   instruments of different colour); it now lies between the horns' C5 and the D in bassoon, violas and Cl 2. The horns double wood-wind and string lines in unison (PDF 106), and the 6/4's doubled bass D4 at 53-54 (c282) is kept.
% c542 (lens 2, Aldwell-Schachter Unit 5 PDF 83, Ex. 5-19: parallel perfect 5ths between two voices are forbidden; PDF 84-85: octave doublings and chord fillers excuse them only when they come from doubling): c541's lead-on F sharp at 48 b3.5 falls to E at 49 in every octave of the tune (F sharp 4/5/6) while Hn 1 and Cl 1 fall B4-A4 on the same downbeat: real 5ths F sharp 5-B4 > E5-A4 (Vln II, Ob 1 against Hn 1, Cl 1) and their compounds against Vln I/Fl 1, f, at the start of the second phrase. Hn 1 and Cl 1 now tie B4 into 49 and fall to A4 on beat 2, a 9-8 suspension over the A bass (A&S Unit 21; the second idea's own tied 9-8 at 54-55): the tune moves over a held note (oblique, PDF 82) and the inner voice moves under a held E6 (oblique). Chord of 49 unchanged from beat 2; 48 unchanged.
sAHnOne = { R2.*28 | fis'2.~\pp\< | fis'2.~ | fis'2.\mf | d'4 r4 r4 | b'2.--(\mf | b'2.-- | a'2-- g'4-- | gis'4-- b'2--~ | b'4 a'2--) | a'2.--( | a'2.-- | a'2.--) | b'2.--( | b'2-- c''4--~ | c''4 b'2--) | b'4-. r4 r4 | }
% c184: 49-52 Hn 2 A3 (was E3), moving to G3 on beat 3 of 52: the E3 sat in unison with the bass at 51 and under the cellos' F sharp 3 at 51 b3-52 b1 (a second against the bass, a ninth E over the D/F sharp chord),
%   and left a void of an 11th-15th between it and A4 in a forte chord (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 82 note: the bass part never mixes with the others; PDF 85-86 and 96: no empty intervals in the middle, especially forte).
%   A3 is a chord tone of A, A7/E, D/F sharp and A7/C sharp, an octave under Hn 1 (concord in one colour, PDF 90 rule 4); G3 on 52 b3 is the seventh of A7, held into the 6/4 at 53 (no octaves with Cl 2's A4-G4 at 52-53).
% c223: 54 Hn 2 A3 (was F sharp 3): the cadential 6/4's fourth G is doubled at the octave at 53 (Vla and Cl 2 G4, Hn 2 G3); one of the doubled fourths now rises
%   (G3-A3-G3) instead of all three falling to F sharp in octaves, and the V7 at 54 no longer doubles the leading tone at the octave (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 316; as Vla E4-F sharp 4 at 354-355).
% c272: 47 Hn 2 A3 (was F sharp 3) with the violas' A4: the passing V of 47 no longer doubles the leading tone F sharp in two inner voices under the melody's F sharp (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 76, Ex. 5-4), as V at 123/279 keeps D sharp in the melody only; 52 Hn 2 holds A3 (was A3-G3): the seventh G of V6/5 of V sounds once, in the violas, which resolve it to F sharp at 54 (PDF 76: the chord seventh is never doubled)
sAHnTwo = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | d'2.~\pp\< | d'2.~ | d'2.\mf | a4 r4 r4 | g2.--(\mf | g2.-- | a2-- g4-- | e2.--) | a2.--( | a2.-- | a2.-- | a2.--) | d'2.--( | d'2-- a'4--~ | a'4 g'2--) | g'4-. r4 r4 | } % c282: bass doubled in the cadential 6/4 (A&S Unit 10 PDF 154)
sATimp = { R2.*7 | r4 b,8\p b,8 e,4 | R2.*7 | r4 b,8\p b,8 e,4 | R2.*26 | g,2.\f~ | g,4-. r4 r4 | }  % c506 (Adler II PDF 79: the timpani consummate the cadence point; PDF 80: show how long the note may ring): the G stroke moves from 56 to the cadence's arrival at 55, with the bass G1, as the E at 131 and 287; it rings through the held G chord and is damped with it at 56 (the c416 damping before the strings-only fugato kept)
% c031: second idea, answer bars 48 and 52 end with a dotted crotchet and an anticipating quaver (d4. e8 | e2; e4. d8 | d4), in Vln I, Vln II, Fl 1, Ob 1; reprises 124/128 and 280/284 likewise (b4. d8 | c2; b4. cis8 | b4)
% c541 (lens 1, Aldwell-Schachter Unit 20 PDF 334-336, Ex. 20-13b: an incomplete neighbour approached by leap decorates the following tone; PDF 336: it belongs in the soprano): 48 the answer's lead-on quaver is F sharp (upper incomplete neighbour of the E at 49, a 9th over E7/G sharp) instead of an anticipating E, so the line no longer strikes E three times (E-D-E | E) and the G-major statement has the same join as the E-minor statements (124, 280: B-D | C). Vln I, Vln II, Fl 1, Ob 1, C.a., Vla, Vc.
sAVlnOne = { e''4-.\p^\markup \italic "leggiero" b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | g''2->( fis''8 f''8 | e''4-.) b'8-. b'-. g'4-. | fis'4-. dis'-. b-. | e''4-. b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | a''2->\dnb( gis''8 g''8 | fis''4-.) dis''8-. dis''-. b'4-. | e''4-. r4 r4 | a''4-. g''-. fis''-. | e''4-. dis''8-. dis''-. e''4-. | fis''4-.\<( g''8 a''8) b''4-. | c'''2->\dnb-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\>( b''8 fis''8) | e''4-.-\tweak stencil ##f \p b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | g''2->\dnb( fis''8 f''8 | e''4-.) dis''8-. dis''-. fis''4-. | e''4-. r4 r4 | R2. | R2. | e''4-. b'8-. b'-. g'4-. | fis'4-. dis'-. b-. | R2. | R2. | fis''4-. d''8-. d''-. a'4-. | d''4-. a'8-. a'-. fis'4-. | g''4-.\< d''8-. d''-. g''4-. | b''2->( bes''8 a''8 | g''4-.) d''8-. d''-. b'4-. | a'4-. fis'-. d'-. | a''4-.\f d''8-. d''-. a''4-. | c'''2->\dnb( b''8 bes''8 | a''4-.) fis''8-. fis''-. d''4-. | a'4-. fis'-. r4 | d'''4.->(\f^\markup \italic "espress."\<\upb c'''8 b''4~ | b''4 g'''2-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\>) | fis'''2( e'''4~ | e'''4-\tweak stencil ##f \f d'''4. fis'''8) | e'''4.->(\< \beatq #-0.4 d'''8 cis'''4~ | cis'''4 a'''2) | g'''2( fis'''4~ | fis'''4 e'''4.) d'''8 | d'''4\ff e'''8( d''' c'''4) | b''2\< a''4~ | a''4-\tweak stencil ##f \fff\> g''2-\tweak stencil ##f \ff | g''4-. r4 r4 | }  % c016: 44 beat 3 rest (was D4): the left hand needs a beat to move from D4 to D6 at 45 (Adler PDF 82-83, wide skips between extreme ranges); Fl 1 and Ob 1 keep the D4
% c073: 16 and 32 Vln II B3 (was A3): theme A's antecedent ends on V, not V7. The melody comes to rest on 5 (F sharp-D sharp-B), and the A3,
%   placed under the melody's low dip, leapt up a seventh to G4 instead of resolving (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 99: V7 is rarely used in a semicadence,
%   V is better when the line rests on 7, 2 or 5; PDF 94-95: the half cadence ends a sentence, the authentic cadence at 20 the paragraph; PDF 98, 104: the 7th resolves down by step).
% c111: 41-43 Vln II plays the tune in unison with Vln I (was off-beat C5/A4, which Cl 1 holds): four winds (Fl 1-2, Ob 1-2, c107) on one string group overpower it (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 76: the strings predominate only when the two are of equal power); the D7 stays complete in Cl, Hn, Bsn and violas. Also 273-275 (dA).
% c134: 52 hidden f closing the swell (MIDI only, prints nothing): with a bare \! the decrescendo from 50 ran in the MIDI to the next printed dynamic, Vln II's p at 65,
%   so the render played the tune's lower octave at velocity 53 (Vln I 83) through the cadence 52-55 (Adler II PDF 159: the melody notes more prominent than the harmony).
% c163: 20 and 28 Vln II G4 on the downbeat of theme A's authentic cadences (were rests): the V7's seventh A4 (Vln II, 19 and 27) resolves down by step, and the cadence's tonic has its third
%   instead of the bare octave E (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 98-99: the 7th of V7 must resolve; PDF 104: each chord needs a root and a 3rd, the 7th resolves down by step; PDF 94: the authentic cadence ends the paragraph).
%   The reprise already does this in the cellos' tenor line (dA 259-260, 267-268: A-G).
% c227: 15-16 and 31-32 Vln II stops on beat 3 of theme A's third bar and rests in its fourth (was r4 g'4 g'4 | r4 b4 b4): the theme's descent B4-G4 | F sharp 4-D sharp 4-B3
%   ended in unison with the off-beats (G4 at 15/31 beat 3, B3 at 16/32 beat 3), bowed staccato in the same colour, so the half cadence's last notes merged into the accompaniment.
%   Adler II PDF 274 (keep the accompanying forces registrally distant from the solo line; rhythmic contrast only lessens the need), PDF 259-260 (the accompaniment neither
%   duplicates nor imitates the solo's rhythm: here it took the melody's own crotchets on its own pitches). The reprise does this already at 271-272 (Vln II r4 g'4 r4, then rest);
%   at 255-256 the off-beats are pizzicato, a different colour from the bowed tune (PDF 257: Beethoven's pizzicato answer avoids duplicating the solo sound), so they stay.
%   The violas' F sharp 3 (16, 32) keeps V complete under the melody; c073's V (not V7) is kept, with no A3 and no B3 doubling the melody.
% c257: 36 Vln II rests on beat 3 and 39 takes G4 on beat 3 (were F sharp 4, B4): the counterstatement's descents D5-A4-A4-F sharp 4 (36, the answer's last note) and G5-D5-D5-B4 (39) met the bowed staccato off-beats in unison, the case c227 fixed at 15-16/31-32; the accompaniment duplicated the focal line's own note and blurred it (Sebesky PDF 12-13: economy, focus). 39's G4 keeps the off-beat pulse into the f at 41.
% c258: 45 "espress." on Vln II, which doubles Vln I's second idea an octave lower at the same f (Sebesky Ex. 281, PDF 214-215).
% c462: 26 beat 3 ii(dim)6 under the theme's F sharp 5 (Vc/Cb pizz. A, Vla C4, Vln II B4-A4; were G bass held, Vln II G4-G4, Vla B3-B3): the fourth phrase's cadence went
%   i6 - cadential 6/4 with the bass arpeggiating G-B, the weakest approach to V (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 135: I-I6-V arpeggiates the stable tones and is less intense than I-II-V;
%   PDF 141 sec. 7-8, Ex. 9-6e, 9-12: I6 leads to II6 and the bass rises by step to V). Now the bass rises G-A-B into the 6/4, the theme's E5 enters the 6/4 by step from II6 as an
%   accented passing tone (PDF 153, Ex. 10-3b), Vln II's A4 is prepared as a common tone for the V7's seventh at 27 (PDF 139, Ex. 9-8a), the violas' C4 falls 6-5 to B3 (PDF 138),
%   and the theme's passing F5 on beat 3.5 sounds over A-C as a Neapolitan sixth. Vln II B4 on beat 2 (was G4) avoids octaves G-A with the bass. The later statements (118, 266, 318)
%   keep the bass G: there the tenor countermelody G3-A3 already steps 3-4 under the same bar.
sAVlnTwo = { r4 g'4-.\pp^\markup \italic "senza sord." g'4-. | r4 g'4-. g'4-. | r4 g'4-. r4 | R2. | r4 g'4-. g'4-. | r4 a'4-. a'4-. | r4 a'4-. a'4-. | g'4-. r4 r4 | r4 a'4-. a'4-. | r4 b'4-. b'4-. | r4 a'4-. a'4-. | r4 a'4-. a'4-. | r4 g'4-. g'4-. | r4 b'4-. a'4-. | r4 a'4-. a'4-. | g'4-. r4 r4 | R2. | R2. | r4 g'4-. r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | r4 fis'4-. fis'4-. | r4 fis'4-. r4 | r4 b'4-.\< b'4-. | r4 b'4-. b'4-. | r4 b'4-. g'4-. | r4 a4-. a4-. | a''4-.\f d''8-. d''-. a''4-. | c'''2->\dnb( b''8 bes''8 | a''4-.) fis''8-. fis''-. d''4-. | r4 c'4-. c'4-. | d''4.->(\f^\markup \italic "espress."\<\upb c''8 b'4~ | b'4 g''2-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\>) | fis''2( e''4~ | e''4-\tweak stencil ##f \f d''4. fis''8) | e''4.->(\< d''8 cis''4~ | cis''4 a''2) | g''2( fis''4~ | fis''4 e''4.) d''8 | d''4\ff e''8( d'' c''4) | b'2\< a'4~ | a'4-\tweak stencil ##f \fff\> g'2-\tweak stencil ##f \ff | g'4-. r4 r4 | }
% c102: 52 violas G4 on the second minim (was E4): A7/C sharp (V6/5 of V), and the G is then held into the cadential 6/4 at 53 as a common tone instead of entering by leap (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 153, 160).
% c207: 40 violas F sharp 3 (was D4): under the theme's dip A4-F sharp 4-D4 the violas sat above Vln II's A3 and on the tune's last note; now each string group keeps its place, Vln II A3 over Vla F sharp 3, as at 16 and 32 (Adler I PDF 135: background out of the melody's register when the colour is the same; PDF 151, Ex. V-26: each instrument in its traditional place in the chord).
% c134: 45 violas mf (were f from 41): their inner line is the harmony layer with Cl 1-2 and Hn 1-2 (mf since c084), one level under the tune (Vln I/II f);
%   Adler II PDF 159 (melody notes more prominent than harmony notes), 184 (principal voice louder than subsidiary ones).
% c265: 13 "unis." at the violas' first entry: movement II (v273) ends with the violas divided (E flat 3 / A flat 3); Adler I PDF 30: when divisi is no longer in order and the part returns to single notes, unis. must appear (as c243's "tutti" for the basses at 3 after movement II's 4 soli; Adler PDF 32).
% c272: 47 violas A4 (was F sharp 4), see sAHnTwo
% c277: 45-46 violas G4-D4-G4 (were G4 held): over the bass G-B-G the I6 at 45 b3-46 b1 had the bass's B in the bass group, Hn 1 and the melody's octaves and its fifth D only in Cl 1,
%   with the violas in unison with Cl 2 on G4; the violas now take the fifth D4 against the bass's third and move each minim as they do at 47, 49 and 51 (Adler I PDF 257, Ex. VIII-25:
%   in a first inversion the third is doubled toward the bottom and the chord kept open; PDF 257: a unison doubling only gives a stronger pitch). Louré dashes kept (c218).
% c323: 27 violas B3 off-beats (were D sharp 4), as at 19: on beats 2-3 of theme A's cadence bar the pizz. B1 has died away and the violas' D sharp 4 was the lowest sounding note, doubling Vln I's D sharp 5 into the tonic at 28 (a root-position vii with doubled leading tone). Now V7 with its root under the melody, as at 19 and as c022 did at 263 (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 292-293, Ex. 18-6; PDF 296 sec. 5).
% c587 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 78: of a soprano melody's two octaves, double the lower one with a string group; PDF 76: Violas + Eng. horn among the best unisons):
%   41-43 the violas leave their off-beat F sharp 4 and play the theme's lower octave with the cor anglais, under Fl/Ob a2 + Vln I/II; F sharp stays in Hn 1 and Cl 2.
sAVla = { r4 b4-.\pp^\markup \italic "senza sord., unis." b4-. | r4 b4-. b4-. | r4 b4-. b4-. | r4 fis4-. fis4-. | r4 b4-. b4-. | r4 c'4-. c'4-. | r4 b4-. b4-. | R2. | r4 c'4-. c'4-. | r4 b4-. b4-. | r4 c'4-. c'4-. | r4 b4-. dis'4-. | r4 b4-. b4-. | r4 b4-. c'4-. | r4 b4-. b4-. | R2. | R2. | R2. | r4 b4-. b4-. | r4 fis4-. fis4-. | R2. | R2. | r4 d'4-. d'4-. | r4 d'4-. d'4-. | r4 d'4-.\< d'4-. | r4 d'4-. d'4-. | r4 d'4-. d'4-. | r4 fis4-. fis4-. | a'4-.\f d'8-. d'-. a'4-. | c''2->\dnb( b'8 bes'8 | a'4-.) fis'8-. fis'-. d'4-. | r4 fis4-. fis4-. | d'4.->(\f^\markup \italic "espress."\<\upb c'8 b4~ | b4 g'2-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\>) | fis'2( e'4~ | e'4-\tweak stencil ##f \f d'4. fis'8) | e'4.->(\< d'8 cis'4~ | cis'4 a'2) | g'2( fis'4~ | fis'4) g'2-- | g'4\ff g'4--( g'4--) | g'2 fis'4 | d'2. | d'4-. r4 r4 | }
% c481 (lens 1, Adler I PDF 89 and 91, Ex. III-39: the 'very successful coupling of violas and cellos' on a tune; PDF 99: the cello's beauty shows in passages for the whole section; PDF 105: a section of basses 'can raise the roof'): 45-55 the cellos leave the bass and sing the second idea's lowest octave (D4-A4, A and D strings) with the violas and the cor anglais, through the hemiola cadence 53-55 where the violas leave it (c419); Bsn 2 takes the cellos' bass line with Bsn 1 (a2), over Cb and Cbsn. 56 cellos back on G2.
